INT. DIM SUM KITCHEN - BACK OFFICE - NIGHT

A cramped room behind the steam. A desk strewn with order slips, a rice cooker, a safe the size of a microwave. Bamboo steamers stack to the ceiling. The kitchen noise thuds through the wall.

LAI MEI-LING (50s, owner, chef's whites, a cleaver stuck in the desk) is counting bills in neat stacks. CAZ DELGADO (30s, her nephew, gold chain, gum) sits on the safe, swinging his heels. MERLE PIKE (60s, accountant, bifocals, mismatched socks) stirs a thermos of tea. PASHA GORSKI (40s, new supplier, expensive coat, a duffel bag at his feet) stands by the door.

LAI
Six thousand.

PASHA
Seven.

LAI
Six. We agreed six.

PASHA
Seven. There was a change.

MERLE
(calmly, to the tea)
There is always a change.

CAZ
Auntie, don't--

LAI
Caz, quiet.

The kitchen noise outside softens. Merle takes a sip. Pasha watches the cleaver in the desk, then the old woman, then the door.

PASHA
You have a nice place.

LAI
It's a restaurant.

PASHA
Your dumplings are very good.

LAI
They are the best.

PASHA
I always say, good food, good business.

CAZ
(dual with Lai)
You don't say anything, you just show up.

LAI
^You say nothing, Mr. Gorski, you take.

They speak at the same moment, in the same tone. Pasha almost laughs.

PASHA
Family, yes? Such a family.

MERLE
Pasha, the number is six.

PASHA
Seven.

MERLE
Six and a half.

PASHA
Mr. Pike--

MERLE
Six and a half, and a case of the shrimp har gow, and you stop coming on Fridays.

PASHA
(smiling)
That is a very strange deal.

LAI
It is a fair deal.

PASHA
Why Fridays?

CAZ
Because that's when the inspector comes.

PASHA
What inspector?

A sound in the wall, a soft thump, like a boot. Merle sets the thermos down. Lai pulls the cleaver out of the desk and lays it across her knees. Caz slides off the safe.

LAI
Mr. Gorski. How many men did you bring tonight?

PASHA
One. Me.

LAI
You should have said two.

PASHA
Why?

The door to the kitchen swings open on its own. A rush of steam rolls through the room, and beyond it the kitchen is entirely, unnaturally still. Not a pan clatters.

CAZ
(whisper)
Auntie. It's Friday.

LAI
I know what day it is.

Pasha kicks his duffel bag. It falls open, and the money inside is stacks of yellow order slips.

MERLE
(sighing)
Six and a half.
